💭 A same-class error-prone generic initializer that directly raises must not leak the dynamically allocated generic
💭 type description it stored on the receiver before failing (#133). A purely inherited initializer, reached by
💭 directly instantiating a subclass that declares neither fields nor an initializer of its own, must not leak it
💭 either: the receiver's runtime class info then never matches the initializer's own owner, so the caller that
💭 requested the object, not the initializer itself, owns the description. A statically known description (no
💭 dynamic generic argument) must never be passed to free(), and an initializer that fails after a successful
💭 explicit superinitializer call must free the description exactly once, from the frame that owns it.

🐇 🐠🐚T⚪🍆 🍇
  🖍🆕 value T

  🆕 v T fail 👌 🚧🚧 🍇
    ↪️ fail 🍇 🚨🆕🚧 🔤failed🔤❗️ 🍉
    v ➡️ 🖍value
  🍉

  ❗️ 🥇 ➡️ T 🍇
    ↩️ value
  🍉
🍉

💭 🐟 declares neither fields nor an initializer, and directly inherits 🐠's error-prone initializer.
🐇 🐟🐚T⚪🍆 🐠🐚T🍆 🍇🍉

💭 🦐 does the same from 🐟, one level further, without declaring an initializer of its own either.
🐇 🦐🐚T⚪🍆 🐟🐚T🍆 🍇🍉

🐇 💰 🍇
  🖍🆕 n 🔢

  🆕 🍇
    0 ➡️ 🖍n
  🍉
🍉

💭 🦑 declares its own field and explicitly calls 🐠's initializer, so the description it received from its caller
💭 must be freed exactly once, by 🦑's own frame, if 🦑 raises after a successful super call.
🐇 🦑🐚T⚪🍆 🐠🐚T🍆 🍇
  🖍🆕 resource 💰

  🆕 v T raise 👌 🚧🚧 🍇
    🆕💰❗️ ➡️ 🖍resource
    ⤴️🆕 v 👎❗️
    ↪️ raise 🍇 🚨🆕🚧 🔤failed🔤❗️ 🍉
  🍉
🍉

